Transports

Transports are translators at the border. Inbound they turn ROS bytes into core messages. Outbound they turn core messages into ROS bytes. Core never sees ROS specifics and ROS peers never see Weld internals.

Interface

Backend is publish bytes, take bytes, join discovery, report liveliness, plus idempotent Close:

- PublishBytes and TakeBytes move uniquely owned payloads; caller must not mutate after send, callee copies if retained, TakeBytes blocks until a message or context done and returns ctx.Err
- JoinDiscovery must precede publish and take; publish or take before join reports ErrNotJoined
- ReportLiveliness carries per-participant generation; greater generation wins, same generation with same aliveness is an idempotent no-op, otherwise ErrStaleGeneration
- After first Close, other methods report ErrBackendClosed
- Backends must be safe for concurrent use

DomainConfig.ResolveDomainID accepts unset and empty as caller value, trims spaces, accepts 042 and +42, rejects non-integral input, overflow, and values above the platform maximum. DiscoveryRange covers not-set, off, localhost, subnet, and system default; NotSet normalizes to system default in the sidecar.

v1 covers topics, services, and actions over the live RTPS backend, all gated. Trigger service calls, param verbs, TF lookup, and action accept plus cancel plus timeout pass live, with extended wire gates for keyed, durability, partitions, writer QoS, content filters, loans, liveliness, and secured payloads (docs/conformance:404).

Code: internal/transports/transport.go.

Naming

- Topics: rt/<name>, requests rq/<name>Request, replies rr/<name>Reply; bare names when avoid-namespace-conventions is set
- Types: :: for double underscore, then <ns>::dds_::<Name>_ with Request_ and Response_ suffixes for services
- Reverse: ParseTopicName handles rt, rq, rr, and bare forms; single-segment rt and exact Request or Reply fall through to topic kind and are documented
- Topic suffix is Reply while type suffix is Response, per ROS naming; this is pinned by a dedicated test

Sidecar

The Sidecar type is a validator stub, not a running peer. NewSidecar
validates the config (domain range, peer address, discovery range),
trims the peer, normalizes the range, and resolves the domain, but
Connect and every Backend data path method return
ErrSidecarNotImplemented; only Close works, as idempotent nil for
teardown. The live backend speaks RTPS directly over UDP with no cgo
and no sidecar process, so Weld builds need no C toolchain and link
no C library. The stub documents that the real backend must honor
context cancellation and require a prior JoinDiscovery.

Code: internal/transports/dds/sidecar.go for the stub,
internal/transports/dds/backend.go for the live RTPS backend.

Post v1

FastDDS, Zenoh, and MQTT land after v1 behind the same interface with interop tests. No plugin registry, no dynamic loading. Core never imports transport packages; the backend interface stays the only crossing.
